.TH OD 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
od \- dump files in octal and other formats
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B od
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ file ...]
.br
.B od \-\-traditional
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ file ]
.RI [ offset
.RI [ label ]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B od
writes the bytes of its input to standard output as numbers or
characters, in one or more formats.
With no format options, it writes 2-byte words in octal.
With no
.I file
operands, or for a
.I file
named
.BR \- ,
it reads standard input.
The files form a single input: addresses run on from one file to the
next, and
.BR \-j ,
.BR \-N ,
and
.B \-S
apply to the whole.
A file that cannot be opened is reported on standard error and skipped,
and the others are still written.
.SS Output
The input is read in blocks, 16 bytes by default; see
.BR \-w .
Each block is written as a line: its address, then its values, each
preceded by a space.
The address is the number of input bytes before the block, counting the
bytes skipped by
.BR \-j .
It has at least 7 octal digits.
After the last block, the address just past the end of the input is
written on a line of its own.
.B \-A
changes the radix of the addresses, or omits them.
.PP
A value of more than one byte is read in the machine's byte order,
which is little-endian, so in hexadecimal the bytes of a word appear
reversed.
The last block may be short.
It is completed with zero bytes to a whole number of values.
.PP
Unless
.B \-v
is given, a block identical to the one before it is not written.
A run of identical blocks is written as its first block followed by a
line holding only
.BR * .
.PP
With several formats, each block is written once for each format, one
line per format.
Only the first line has the address, and the others begin with spaces in
its place.
The formats are written in a fixed order, not the order given:
.BR \-a ;
.BR \-b ;
.BR \-c ;
.BR \-d ;
.BR \-D ;
.BR \-f ;
.B \-h
or
.BR \-x ;
.B \-H
or
.BR \-X ;
.BR \-i ,
.BR \-I ,
.BR \-l ,
or
.BR \-L ;
.B \-o
or
.BR \-B ;
.BR \-O ;
the
.B \-t
types, in the order given; and
.BR \-s .
.SS Type specifiers
The argument of
.B \-t
is made of type specifiers.
A specifier is a type letter, an optional size, and an optional
.BR z .
Each value is written after a space, in a field as wide as the largest
value of its type and size: octal and hexadecimal values are padded with
zeros, and decimal values with spaces.
.TP
.BR d ", " o ", " u ", " x
An integer in signed decimal, octal, unsigned decimal, or hexadecimal.
The size is
.B C
for 1 byte,
.B S
for 2,
.B I
for 4,
.B L
for 4 as well, since a Windows
.B long
has 4 bytes, or the number 1, 2, 4, or 8.
The default is 4.
.TP
.B f
A floating-point number in exponent notation, with 7 digits after the
point for 4-byte values and 15 for 8-byte values.
The size is
.B F
for 4 bytes, a
.BR float ;
.B D
for 8, a
.BR double ;
.B L
for a
.BR "long double" ,
16 bytes on x64 and 12 on x86; or one of those numbers.
The default is 8.
See
.B NOTES
about
.BR "long double" .
.TP
.B a
A named character, 4 columns wide.
The byte is taken modulo 128.
Printable characters other than space are written as themselves.
Space is
.BR sp ,
0x7f is
.BR del ,
and each control character is its lowercase ASCII name, such as
.BR nul ,
.BR bel ,
or
.BR esc ,
except that LF is
.BR nl .
.TP
.B c
A character, 4 columns wide.
Printable ASCII, 0x20 to 0x7e, is written as itself.
NUL, BEL, BS, FF, LF, CR, tab, and VT are written as
.BR \e0 ,
.BR \ea ,
.BR \eb ,
.BR \ef ,
.BR \en ,
.BR \er ,
.BR \et ,
and
.BR \ev .
Any other byte is written as 3 octal digits.
.PP
The types
.B a
and
.B c
take no size.
A
.B z
after a specifier adds to the end of each of its lines the bytes of the
block as text between
.B >
and
.BR < ,
with a period for each byte that is not printable ASCII.
.PP
Several specifiers may follow one another in a
.B \-t
argument, as in
.BR dCdS ,
but a numeric size must end the argument.
A letter after it, such as the
.B z
in
.B x1z
or the second
.B d
in
.BR d2d2 ,
gives a misleading error about a 4294967295-byte type.
Give the size as a letter, as in
.BR xCz ,
or give
.B \-t
again.
.SS Traditional syntax
Without
.BR \-\-traditional ,
every operand is a file.
With it, at most one file may be given, and the operands can also be an
offset, which is the number of bytes to skip and replaces any
.BR \-j ,
and after an offset a label.
.PP
An offset is octal, or decimal when it contains a period, or
hexadecimal when it starts with
.B 0x
or
.BR 0X .
The period ends the number, and anything after it is ignored.
A leading
.B +
is ignored.
A
.B b
or
.B B
at the end of an octal or decimal number multiplies it by 512 or 1024,
as in
.B 1b
or
.BR 2b. ;
in a hexadecimal number they are digits.
.PP
One operand is an offset or a file.
Two operands are an offset and a label, or a file and an offset.
Three are a file, an offset, and a label.
Anything else is an error.
Where an offset may appear, an operand that starts with a digit, after
any
.BR + ,
is taken as one, so
.B od \-\-traditional 1.txt
skips 1 byte of standard input, and a malformed offset such as
.B 1file
is an error.
.PP
The label, written like an offset, is the address to show for the first
byte written.
Each address is then followed by the label address in parentheses.
With
.BR "\-A n" ,
only an address in parentheses is written, and it is the plain address,
not the label address.
.SH OPTIONS
Options may be grouped, as in
.BR \-bx ,
and may follow the
.I file
operands.
An option's argument may be attached, as in
.BR \-N16 ,
or be the next argument.
The argument of a long option may follow an
.B =
or be the next argument.
The exceptions are
.BR \-w ,
.BR \-\-width ,
and
.BR \-\-strings ,
whose argument is optional and must be attached, as in
.B \-w8
or
.BR \-\-strings=8 .
Long options may be abbreviated to a unique prefix.
.B \-\-
ends the options.
.PP
The arguments of
.BR \-j ,
.BR \-N ,
and
.B \-S
are numbers in decimal, hexadecimal with a
.B 0x
prefix, or octal with a leading
.BR 0 .
A lowercase
.BR b ,
.BR k ,
or
.B m
after the number multiplies it by 512, 1024, or 1048576.
A sign is not allowed.
.TP
.B \-a
Same as
.BR "\-t a" .
.TP
.B \-b
Same as
.BR "\-t oC" :
octal bytes.
.TP
.B \-c
Same as
.BR "\-t c" .
.TP
.B \-d
Same as
.BR "\-t u2" :
unsigned decimal 2-byte words.
.TP
.B \-D
Same as
.BR "\-t u4" .
.TP
.B \-f
Same as
.BR "\-t fF" :
4-byte floating point.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-x
Same as
.BR "\-t x2" :
hexadecimal 2-byte words.
.TP
.BR \-H ", " \-X
Same as
.BR "\-t x4" .
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-I ", " \-l ", " \-L
Same as
.BR "\-t d4" :
signed decimal 4-byte integers.
All four are the same here; see
.BR NOTES .
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-B
Same as
.BR "\-t o2" :
octal 2-byte words.
This is the default when no format is given.
.TP
.B \-O
Same as
.BR "\-t o4" .
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-output\-duplicates
Write every block, including those identical to the one before.
.TP
.B \-s
Same as
.BR "\-t d2" :
signed decimal 2-byte words.
.TP
\fB\-t\fR \fItype\fR, \fB\-\-format=\fR\fItype\fR
Add the format or formats that
.I type
describes; see
.BR "Type specifiers" .
May be repeated.
An invalid
.I type
is an error.
.TP
\fB\-A\fR \fIradix\fR, \fB\-\-address\-radix=\fR\fIradix\fR
Write addresses in the radix named by the first character of
.IR radix :
.B d
for decimal, with at least 7 digits;
.B o
for octal, with at least 7 digits, the default;
.B x
for hexadecimal, with at least 6 lowercase digits;
or
.B n
for none, which also omits the final address.
Any other character is an error, but an empty
.I radix
is accepted and garbles the addresses.
.TP
\fB\-N\fR \fIbytes\fR, \fB\-\-read\-bytes=\fR\fIbytes\fR
Read at most
.I bytes
bytes of input, counted after the
.B \-j
skip.
Less is read if the input ends first.
If the skip and
.I bytes
together overflow 63 bits, it is an error.
.TP
\fB\-j\fR \fIbytes\fR, \fB\-\-skip\-bytes=\fR\fIbytes\fR
Skip
.I bytes
bytes of input before writing.
Within regular files the skip seeks, and a regular file wholly within it
is not read; other input is read and discarded.
A skip past the end of the combined input is an error, and nothing is
written.
A skip of 2 GiB or more into a regular file goes wrong; see
.BR NOTES .
.TP
\fB\-S\fR \fIlength\fR, \fB\-\-strings\fR[\fB=\fR\fIlength\fR]
Write strings instead of values.
A string is a run of at least
.I length
printable ASCII characters, 0x20 to 0x7e, ended by a NUL byte.
The default
.I length
is 3.
Each string is written on a line of its own, after its address and a
space, and no final address is written.
Any other byte, such as a tab, ends a run without a string, and so does
the end of the input.
Formats are checked but not otherwise used.
The argument of
.B \-S
is required.
.TP
\fB\-w\fR[\fIbytes\fR], \fB\-\-width\fR[\fB=\fR\fIbytes\fR]
Write
.I bytes
bytes of input per line, in decimal.
Without a number, the width is 32.
Without
.BR \-w ,
it is 16, or the least common multiple of the value sizes when that
does not divide 16, as with a 12-byte
.B long double
on x86.
The width must be a positive multiple of the size of every value.
If it is not,
.B od
warns
.B invalid width
on standard error and uses the least common multiple of the sizes
instead.
.TP
.B \-\-traditional
Accept the operands described under
.BR "Traditional syntax" .
.TP
.B \-\-help
As the first argument, print a usage message on standard output and
exit.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B POSIXLY_CORRECT
When set, the first
.I file
operand ends the options, so that later arguments such as
.B \-c
are file names.
.TP
.B BB_GLOBBING
Unless it is
.BR 0 ,
wildcards in the operands are expanded, as needed when run from
.BR cmd.exe .
A busybox process started with it unset sets it to
.B 0
for its children, so commands run from the kit's shell receive their
operands as the shell expanded them.
.PP
Variables that affect every busybox applet are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
An invalid option, number, address radix, type, or operand; a skip past
the end of the input; a failed seek; a read or write error; or a file
that could not be opened.
.TP
.B 141
Standard output is a pipe that was closed before all output was written.
.PP
A file that cannot be opened or read is reported, and
.B od
goes on with the next file.
A failed seek, described in
.BR NOTES ,
is not reported, and
.B od
goes on from the start of the file.
Either way the exit status is 1.
The other errors stop
.B od
at once.
.SH EXAMPLES
Dump a file in the default format.
Each word is read little-endian, so the bytes 0x48 0x65 of
.B He
appear as 062510:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ od hello.txt
0000000 062510 066154 026157 073440 071157 062154 005041
0000016
.EE
.RE
.PP
Write the bytes in hexadecimal, with no addresses:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ od \-An \-tx1 hello.txt
 48 65 6c 6c 6f 2c 20 77 6f 72 6c 64 21 0a
.EE
.RE
.PP
Write hexadecimal addresses, hexadecimal bytes, and the bytes as text,
8 bytes per line:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ od \-Ax \-t xCz \-w8 hello.txt
000000 48 65 6c 6c 6f 2c 20 77  >Hello, w<
000008 6f 72 6c 64 21 0a        >orld!.<
00000e
.EE
.RE
.PP
Print the 4-byte value at offset 0x3c of an EXE, the offset of its PE
header:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ od \-An \-tu4 \-j 0x3c \-N 4 xxd.exe
        128
.EE
.RE
.PP
List the strings of at least 8 characters in a program, with their
addresses in hexadecimal:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ od \-Ax \-S 8 program.exe
.EE
.RE
.PP
Read 4-byte floating-point numbers:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ printf \(aq\e0\e0\e200\e077\(aq | od \-An \-tfF
  1.0000000e+000
.EE
.RE
.PP
Skip the first 7 bytes with the obsolete operand syntax, in which the
offset is octal:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ od \-\-traditional \-tx1 hello.txt 7
0000007 77 6f 72 6c 64 21 0a
0000016
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Unlike
.B od
on 64-bit Unix systems,
.BR \-I ,
.BR \-l ,
.BR \-L ,
and the size
.B L
are 4 bytes, the size of a Windows
.BR long ,
so they match
.B \-i
and
.BR "\-t d4" .
Use the size 8 for 8-byte integers, as in
.BR "\-t d8" .
.PP
The obsolete operands that POSIX describes are accepted only with
.BR \-\-traditional .
.PP
Floating-point numbers are formatted by the Microsoft C runtime, so
exponents have three digits, as in
.BR 1.0000000e+000 ,
and infinities and NaNs are written in forms such as
.B 1.#INF000e+000
and
.BR 1.#QNAN00e+000 .
That runtime cannot format a
.BR "long double" ,
so the types
.B fL
and
.B f16
(or
.B f12
on x86) write wrong values.
Use
.B fD
for doubles.
.PP
With
.B \-A n
and more than one format, the lines after the first for each block are
indented by an arbitrary number of columns, because the width of the
missing address is read from past the end of a table.
.PP
With
.BR \-S ,
a string cut off by the limit of
.B \-N
is written, but with an address one less than its own.
.PP
With
.B \-N
and no
.BR \-S ,
input is read without buffering, so a program that shares standard input
with
.B od
continues where
.B od
stopped.
.PP
Addresses, and the numbers given to
.B \-j
and
.BR \-N ,
are 64-bit, but a skip that reaches 2 GiB or more into a regular file
seeks with only the low 32 bits of the offset, as a signed number.
When those bits are negative the seek fails, and
.B od
dumps from the start of the file and exits with status 1, without a
message; otherwise it dumps from the wrong place.
Either way the addresses show the requested skip.
A pipe is skipped by reading, so
.B cat
.I file
.B | od \-j
.I bytes
works.
.PP
Input from files and pipes is read as bytes: a CR LF is not translated,
and Ctrl-Z does not end the input.
Output lines end with LF.
.PP
File names may use drive letters and either kind of slash.
They may contain any Unicode characters on Windows 10 version 1903 and
later, and are limited to the system code page on earlier versions.
The names
.IR /dev/null ,
.IR /dev/zero ,
.IR /dev/urandom ,
.IR /dev/stdin ,
.IR /dev/tty ,
and
.I /dev/fd/N
work as input files, but after reading
.IR /dev/fd/0 ,
.B od
fails with
.B "standard input: Bad file descriptor"
and exits with status 1.
A directory fails with
.BR "Is a directory" .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR hexdump (1),
.BR xxd (1)
